-- chem/pairgauge.cub — every atom's lower end from one two-electron operator (docs/chemical-identity.md, "The
-- lower bound is one square over a solved base"; the working state is in the artifact "Exact chemistry from quantum
-- geometry, as proved programs"). The object is
--   K[ζ, γ, δ] = h_ζ(1) + h_ζ(2) + 1/r₁₂ − γ·(p₁ + p₂) + δ·p₁p₂,
-- h_ζ hydrogen's operator at charge ζ, p the projector on one spatial orbital, with ε_s and ε_t its lowest singlet and
-- triplet levels. For an atom of charge Z with N electrons and spin S, and every c ≥ 0,
--   E(Z, N, S) ≥ (N−1)·[n_s·ε_s + n_t·ε_t]   at   ζ = Z/(N−1), γ = c/(N−1)², δ = 2c/(N−1)        (`pg-bound`).
-- The reasoning, each step one identity below. (1) A spatial orbital holds n ∈ {0, 1, 2} electrons, so n(2−n) ≥ 0
-- there (`pg-occ-vals`), and n(2−n) = n − 2·(pairs sharing the orbital), the pairs being n(n−1)/2 (`pg-occ`): H minus
-- c times it is H with that orbital lowered by c and a repulsion 2c between two electrons sharing it, and is below H.
-- (2) A one-electron term is shared by the N−1 pairs its electron is in, so the operator is a sum over pairs; with
-- lengths divided by N−1 a pair's operator is (N−1)·K at the parameters above (`pg-scale`: δ = 2(N−1)γ; `pg-shift`:
-- a pair with both electrons in the orbital moves by 2c − 2c/(N−1), a pair with one by −c/(N−1)). (3) An
-- antisymmetric state of spin S restricted to a pair is a mixture of singlets and triplets with n_s + n_t = N(N−1)/2
-- and S(S+1) = 3N/4 + (n_t − 3n_s)/2, which fixes n_s = N(N+2)/8 − S(S+1)/2 (`pg-counts`; lithium 3/2 and 3/2,
-- `pg-li`; two electrons in a singlet 1 and 0, where the bound is ε_s itself, `pg-two`). (4) The tail of the block
-- inequality (chem/tailblock.cub): with n the number of electrons in a finite orbital set, the complement of
-- "all N inside" has n ≤ N−1, and for a state there with ⟨n⟩ = m and any lower bound L of H + κ·n,
--   (L′ − κ·m) − (L − κ·(N−1)) = (L′ − L) + κ·(N−1−m)                                        (`pg-tail`)
-- with L′ = ⟨H + κ·n⟩ ≥ L: so ⟨H⟩ ≥ L − κ(N−1) on the complement, for every κ ≥ 0. H + κ·n is of the same kind
-- (levels of the set raised by κ), so (1)–(3) bound it too. Taken here, not proved: the mixture in (3); that the
-- sum of the pairs' lowest levels bounds the sum's lowest level; the operator statements behind (1), (2), (4) on
-- the function class. ε_s and ε_t are arguments here: any certified lower bounds of them give a certified bound.
import "tailblock.hypercubical"

